          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="12" colspan="7"> 
              <div align="center"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><b>The 
                Questions</b></font></div>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="4"> 
              <hr size="1">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"> 
              <div class="Section1"> 
                <h2 style="margin-right:1.0in">&nbsp;</h2>
                <div align="center"> </div>
                <h2 style="margin-right:1.0in"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><o:p></o:p></font></h2>
              </div>
            </td>
            <td height="5" width="47%"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">1) Israel 
                has been "cracking down" against terrorism for three decades now, 
                and yet to this day suicide bombings continue to take place -- 
                why does Israel continue following a policy that is patently a 
                failure? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">2) If Israel 
                were serious about a long-lasting peace with the Palestinians, 
                then why did it double the size of its settlements in the occupied 
                territories between 1993 and 2000? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">3) If Palestinians 
                were only intent on destroying Israel, then why did attacks against 
                Israelis taper off to nothing when Barak seemed to be making progress 
                towards peace? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">4) If Israel 
                really wants Arafat to reign in militants, as it says it does, 
                then why does it make it both politically and physically impossible 
                for him to do so by humiliating him, denouncing him as a criminal, 
                and physically dismantling his security apparatus and the infrastructure 
                of his authority? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">5) If Israel 
                were really interested in ensuring the security of its citizens, 
                then why does it carry out assassinations, knowing full well that 
                such assassinations will only trigger another wave of suicide 
                bombings and escalate the cycle of violence? </font></p>
            </td>
            <td height="5" colspan="2"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="45%"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">1) 
                Why does the PA only crack down on overt terrorist groups and 
                their well-known leaders when forced to by external forces or 
                events? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">2) 
                Why don't Palestinians admit that many of Israel's security concerns 
                are real and must be addressed as a major part of any peace? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">3) 
                Why haven't the Palestinians truly given passive, non-violent 
                resistance a real, sustained try in their entire history, vis-à-vis 
                Israel (particularly since other nations and slighted groups have 
                done so in history)? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">4) 
                Israel was founded as a Jewish State, a homeland for a long mistreated 
                and maligned people. If Palestinians were truly willing to accept 
                Israel as a permanent neighbor, then why continue to push the 
                antithetical position that Zionism is racism? </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3">5) 
                If a peace agreement with Israel entailed the following - secure 
                and contiguous borders, fair access to water, dismantling of some 
                or all settlements, a shared Jerusalem and possibly some monetary 
                compensation - would the Palestinian people be willing to give 
                up the right of return? In other words, is the right of return 
                negotiable or inviolable? </font></p>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="4"> 
              <hr size="1">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="4"> 
              <div align="center"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><b>The 
                Answers </b></font></div>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="4"> 
              <hr size="1">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="47%"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">1) The PA 
                does harshly "crack down" on militants, risking civil war and 
                internecine strife, when hellfire is unleashed against it; but 
                we must keep in mind that the best security results -- almost 
                a whole year of no attacks on Israel during Barak's administration 
                -- were obtained when the Israelis and Palestinians were cooperating 
                with one another as partners. Arafat may appease the Israelis 
                by doing what he does best -- jail his opposition -- but to think 
                that forcing him to do so by destroying his helicopters and flattening 
                his police stations will bring about a more stable security situation 
                is a bit far fetched. </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">2) The Palestinians 
                HAVE admitted that Israel has security concerns and have said 
                as much repeatedly. Here is a quote from an official memorandum 
                released by the Palestinian Negotiating Team in January, 2001: 
                "The original Oslo Accords had mandated that the peace talks be 
                concluded three years ago with a Palestinian state and an Israeli 
                state living in freedom, security, and equality side by side." 
                Given that the Palestinians are signatories to the Oslo Accords, 
                it follows that they have officially admitted and accepted "that 
                many of Israel's security concerns are real and must be addressed 
                as a major part of any peace." </font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">3) To be sure, 
                the non-violent option has not been pursued as energetically as 
                it should. But here my answer is two-fold: (a) The Israel occupation 
                has done its darnest to quell any movement toward non-violent 
                resistance. More than once, Palestinians have for instance attempted 
                to protest by not paying taxes. Israel reacted each time by "cracking 
                down" on them as if they were terrorists; and (b) Visit Hanan 
                Ashrawi's Miftah group, <a href="http://www.miftah.org" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">http://www.miftah.org</a> 
                , for an example of a growing effort to push forward a non-violent 
                resistance movement with a strategy for the future. No one talks 
                about such efforts -- certainly not the US media -- since they 
                don't fit the easy caricature of mad, violent Palestinians.</font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">4) The proposition 
                that Zionism is a form of racism may be jarring to those who champion 
                Zionism, but it is still the moral duty of the world to point 
                out that a state that explicitly -- via its law of return for 
                Jews, for instance, or its stated policy of increasing the Jewish 
                population in Israel, or its practice of denying Arabs the appropriation 
                of property -- promotes the welfare of one specific ethnic group 
                at the expense of another is engaging in racism. Moreover, the 
                racism issue is not a side question, but goes to the heart of 
                a VERY important piece of the puzzle towards peace: the right 
                of Palestinian refugees and their descendents driven from their 
                homes in 1948 and later, to return home. Israel has time and again 
                objected to their return not by arguing that they are not legally 
                entitled to reclaim their property, but by saying that if they 
                were allowed to return, the Jewish character of Israel would be 
                upset. That is patently a racist policy and a fundamental road 
                block to peace. The real question is: how can Israel pursue Zionist 
                goals and at the same time claim to be an egalitarian, liberal 
                modern democracy?</font></p>
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">5) Only those 
                whose land, houses, and property, were illegally taken over by 
                the Israeli state have the right to negotiate over their right 
                of return. Any deal MUST take their respective voices into consideration, 
                and no one has the right to speak on their behalf without their 
                assent, be it the Palestinian Authority, the United Nations, or 
                any other legal entity. I am certain that a morally acceptable 
                solution to this problem does exist. The question is: does Israel 
                have the will to consider such solutions, or is ITS rejection 
                of any right of Palestinian return claims -- as stated by Barak 
                in the height of his "generosity" -- categorical and non-negotiable. 
                </font></p>
            </td>
            <td height="5" colspan="2"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="45%"> <font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 1) Israelis 
                are imperfect human beings, just like everyone else. Due to the 
                endless series of actions and reactions, Israel is now seemingly 
                "locked" into this position. Of course, you could argue that there 
                would have been countless more suicide bombings had there been 
                no response by Israel. One supporting bit of evidence about this 
                is that there is no airline that cracks down on potential terrorist 
                activity better than El Al, and lo and behold, that airline is 
                the safest in the world. So maybe the policy is not a failure. 
                But, it is true that it cannot be defined as a success either. 
                </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 2) I have 
                no supportive comments for the settlements. Unfortunately, there 
                are people in Israel that believe very passionately in that activity. 
                I believe that they are wrong. I believe that their presence is 
                an unnecessary barrier now to peace. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 3) I think 
                that this question brings up two separate issues. First, there 
                is the issue of whether the Palestinians want to destroy Israel. 
                I would like to believe that this is no longer the case amongst 
                the group of Palestinians that are truly peace-desiring, however 
                large that group is. I am not completely convinced, unfortunately. 
                One reason is the longstanding policy stated in the PLO charter 
                which speaks again and again about basically destroying Israel. 
                Now this is an old charter, but why was that theme there in the 
                first place? A second reason is Palestinian insistence on the 
                full right of return, which would be a demographic, political 
                way of irrevocably changing Israel. The final reason is the championing 
                of the proposition that Zionism is racism, which is yet another 
                way of indirectly attacking the underpinnings of the Jewish state. 
                </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> The second 
                part of the question is the issue of the frequency of attacks. 
                I am not as familiar as I would like to be about the accounting 
                of Palestinian attacks on Israel. If the frequency did indeed 
                decrease during this period, then that is good. It certainly proves 
                that the forces that want peace have the ability to control the 
                frequency of such attacks. One wonders why this control doesn't 
                always apply. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 4) There 
                are times where it appears that Israel is simultaneously mistreating 
                Arafat and destabilizing him, while also asking him to come through. 
                At those moments, that doesn't seem fair. On the other hand, I 
                believe that Arafat has an abysmal record of following through 
                on real efforts to peace and that is very frustrating. He could 
                be described quite accurately as a coward, a corrupt leader, a 
                terrorist himself (see countless PLO activities that he personally 
                led in the past). Let's see if he comes through this time, particularly 
                with Egypt, Jordan and even Saudi Arabia now pressuring him publicly. 
                </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 5) I agree 
                that the cycle of violence is out of hand and is leading everyone 
                down the wrong road. And when children die, especially, it is 
                truly tragic. It has become almost irrelevant who started it. 
                Israel seems to me to resort to assassinations of people known 
                to be masterminds behind suicide bombing on Israeli soil. They 
                do this because it feels that the Palestinian Authority won't 
                do its job, as a governing body should. The men that the Israelis 
                try to kill are criminals, killers, cowards. Once again, who knows 
                how many more Israelis would be killed if Israel did nothing. 
                I guess that you could also ask, why do Palestinian leaders think 
                that it is enough to express condolences for innocent Israelis 
                killed by suicide bombings, when they do very little of a permanent 
                nature to eliminate the forces that perpetuate the terrorism (even 
                though those people are well-known to all). </font>
              </p></font><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 
              </font></p></td>
          </tr>
