            </td>
          </tr>

          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="47%"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="2"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="45%"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="3"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="47%"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">1) I sympathize 
                with the Israelis' need to ensure their security and I recognize 
                the historical wrongs suffered upon Jews in general. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">2) I admire 
                the Israelis' respect for freedom of expression: Israeli newspapers, 
                for instance, are far more informative and accepting of dissent 
                than are American newspapers. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 3) I respect 
                how the Israelis were able to resurrect a virtually dead language 
                - Hebrew.</font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 4) I respect 
                the Israelis' technological sophistication (minus their military 
                war machine)</font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 5) To the 
                extent that the Israelis respect the rule of law and to the extent 
                that they abide by the principles of democratic participation 
                and inclusion, I do admire their democratic society. I also admire 
                the various Israeli peace-seeking groups and those who have stood 
                up against oppression and injustice, and more importantly, I respect 
                the fact that they can engage in such activities without fear 
                from despotic repression -- which is much more than I can say 
                for the Arab and Muslim regimes of today.</font>
            </p></td>
            <td height="5" colspan="2"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"></font></td>
            <td height="5" width="45%"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">1) I respect 
                the high level of education amongst the Palestinian people. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 2) I respect 
                the perseverance to achieve nationhood. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 3) I sympathize 
                with those who lose loved ones in the conflict very much, particularly 
                the innocents. I also sympathize with those parents who see their 
                children become lost to the dark side in the struggle. I sympathize 
                with those who lost their property in the late 40's as a result 
                of being forced to leave. They deserve some compensation. </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 4) I sympathize 
                with the very restricted life that is imposed due to the conflict 
                - the roadblocks, the lost economic and educational opportunities. 
                </font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> 5) I feel 
                very badly for those Palestinians who foresaw a building of democratic 
                institutions in their territories as a result of Oslo, and instead 
                got despotic, corrupt government instead. </font>
            </p></td>
          </tr>
