			<tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="5" colspan="7"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">
			<hr height="1"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><font size="2" face="Verdana"></font></font></font></font><table align="center" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="88%"><tbody><tr><td><a href="mailto:mjeser@jccgb.org" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Michael Jeser</a> [12/21/2001 11:10:03 AM]<br><br>The need to engage in open and reasonable debate is unquestionable.  My issue lies in the fear of many Israelis, and not unfounded, that they face an existential threat.  Less than twenty years ago, there were Arab leaders promising to bush the Jews into the sea.  Israelis fear that every inch they give will only lead to a greater desire to take over all of Israel.  This is not to ignore the Palestinian fear of what the settlements represent.  They are two separate issues and the latter is one that continues to be a thorn for Israel.  The major difference is that Israeli policy has never been one of domination.  Settlements were not established by an aggressive act.  They were in response to a war that was forced upon Israel.  I guess my question here is why don't people ever question resolution 242?  Don't people remember the context of which these settlements were established? I do not support the settlements, but fear that the withdrawel wil just bring the middle east to another boiling point, and one that Israel will not be able to defend.  Israel was willing to live on the 1948 partition plan, but it's neighbours were not.  It's not such a far out suggestion that a policy in the PA exists that means to destroy the stae of Israel.  This is my fear.  I want a just solution, but am learning to understand that the two sides imagine two different just solutions.  In response to the partition of Israel, the Palestinian cause may not stop at a two state settlement.  do people really think that Palestinians will be able to one day return to their homes in Haifa, Jaffo and Tel Aviv?  If this has been promised to these people, i fear that this war will go on.  Unfortunately, the people that impact policy are not the peace seeking majority.  Hamas and Islamc Jihad run the show and essentially exploit the weakness of the current regime.  I can only pray that the good intentioned sides from both peoples will shine through. <br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:mary339us@yahoo.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">mary quan</a> [12/21/2001 2:53:16 PM]<br><br>I am in favor of a two state solution.  I feel that the Israelis should without question move back to the 1967 borders and give equal access to water.  Also, the Palestinians should have full sovereignty
over their skies.  In return the State of Israel should finally be able to enjoy peace and live free from suicide bombings.  Jerusalem should not go fully to either party but should be either split or put 
under international rule.  In terms of the right of return, this is an unreasonable demand.  Some Palestinians should be allowed to return; I don't know what basis that would be determined on, but for the rest
there should be incentive enough to move and find new homes, with  fair compensation provided at least in part by Israel.  
My biggest fear however is that this resolution will come too late.  If the two parties do not sit down at the table soon, I am afraid that when they do settle their differences they will be so alienated and mistrusting of each other that 
they will enjoy a relationship that contains constant hostility and atagonism.  This point may have already been reached, but dialogue like this must come soon..
Major complaints I have for both sides is- a non-violent peace movement would have gained a thousand fold more support than what the Palestinians have now.  Also, I feel the Israeli government is guilty of lying (deliebrately, or 
not) to their people and us Americans by exploiting their fears of destruction and anti-semtism, but also by isolating the Palestinians so that they cannot be allowed to express themselves.  It is intolerable that 
the leader of the Palestinian people is virtually under house arrest right now and not able to travel to Bethlehem without permission.  How is peace supposed to begin with these right wing zealots in power, and being heavily supported by the
settlers.  






























<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:tabarani@mailcity.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Ahmad Al-Tabarani</a> [12/21/2001 3:40:58 PM]<br><br>*Israel gives the right to any Jew to settle in Israel but it denies the right of expelled Palestinians to return to their land that they were kicked out of it in the 1948 Arab Israeli war.
Being the nation that suffered most on the face of the earth doesn't give you the right to get reach your rights by denying the others. 
The Might Is Right principle that the Israelis are using right now will turn against them one day.
<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:lutz-dettinger@fachklinik-hohenstuecken.de" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Nikolaus Lutz-Dettinger</a> [12/21/2001 5:35:54 PM]<br><br>I wonder, what Michael Jeser means by stating that "the major difference is that Israeli policy has never been one of domination". Indeed, with the new cycle of violence now it is easy to claim, that the Israelis keep the settlements out of fear, for protection of the mere existence of Israel. But it should not be forgotten that immediately preceding the second intifada Israeli politicians were honest enough to admit, that they kept the settlements not for this reason, but because they needed them for their immigrants; it is completely unclear to me, how there can be any Israeli or anybody anyhow, who isn't able to see the utter criminality of this position.
I agree with the opinion that the demand for an absolute right to return (for Palestinians or for anybody else in the world) can only lead to a never ending succession of wars. Instead of "justice", which goes back in history indefinitely, we must try to accept old injustices and only correct the most recent ones. In other words: the palestinians have to accept the existence of Israel within its pre-1967-borders (and if they want to live there: accept that they live in Israel and have to live according to the rules of that country), while Israel has to retreat completely to the pre-1967-situation (and if Israelis want to stay outside these borders in their settlements, they must accept that they live in the state of Palestine and that they have to live according to the rules of that country). Of course, as any modern state, both the state of Israel and the state of Palestine should have a reasonably open policy towards foreigners, especially those who are already living in their country for years or for ever. Jerusalem should probably as a whole get international status, but while what I said higher up seems extremely straight forward and self-evident to me, I don't feel sure about the best solution for Jerusalem.
General conclusion: refrain from seeking absolute justice the more, the longer ago the injustice happened. Because, the longer ago the injustice happened, the more will it be impossible to get justice without inflicting new injustice at the same time.
Greetings, a belated Eid mubarak, joyful Christmas and a happy new year,
Dr. Nikolaus Lutz-Dettinger<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:ga948@aol.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Suzanne Moorman Abdulhay</a> [12/21/2001 6:01:19 PM]<br><br>The United States of America has funded the Jewish state of Israel for many years with billions of dollars making it the most powerful country in the Middle East and beyond.  Israel is an incomparable occupier, with a bottomless bank roll, (today it has purchased 50 brand new F-l6's from Lockheed Martin) who has no country to answer to but the United States.  All of the power, all of the weapons of mass destruction, all of the money is in the hands of the state of Israel. In my opinion, the blame rests squarely on the shoulders of Israel, for with the power they have had the solutions to this conflict for many years.  Treat the Palestinians as brothers who are as much a part of that land as they are.  Now, when I look at some of the things that they do to the Palestinians, I am reminded of what was done to them not even 60 years ago by racists of another time wanting power.   

America and Israel could have peace tomorrow if they wanted it. But they have all the power and they don't have to listen to the Palestinians.  As Americans, we give Palestinians no voice to tell their side of the story.  With no microphone and no pen (or no one who will air your work or print your article) bombs say something.  With no hope left, what the heck, a suicide bomb will say even more.  It's time to listen to what the Palestinian has to say.
<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:nissir@btinternet.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Nisrine Barakeh</a> [12/21/2001 6:01:33 PM]<br><br>The problem is that the root of the issues is never addressed by the Israelis.  Certain truths are totally ignored They occupied Palestine in 1948.  They practiced and continue to practice aggressive ethnic cleansing.  They practically moved into houses which Palestinians were forced to evacuate under agression or the threat thereof (and thats the least of their crimes).  Their claim is based solely on 'might is right' therefore and not on any moral grounds.  I often wonder at the psychological make up of an Israeli.  He knows he stole a country.  He knows he doesnt have a moral right in even the air that he breathes in that country, very likely in a property where its inhabitants have been killed or worse, yet it seems he  continues to live in Palestine48 quiet comfortably.  He can do so in only 2 ways:  he is horribly and willfully ignorant; or that he is really, a very immoral human being.  Otherwise, he would be battling for Palestinian rights, as indeed a very small number of israelis seem to be doing (e.g Israel Shamir)or he would leave and refuse to be any part of such an occupation.   The zionist racist excuse is all but totally discredited.  Israel as sitting on top of a bloody Palestine, is only an extention of the European colonialist practices taken one step further, where the native people of a country counted for nothing.  This in a nutshell is what Israel is all about.  Israelis cannot in truth convince any sane human being, that its the will of God that they kill, rape, destroy, displace and steal, all in his name and for his sake.
The Israelis and the Palestinian so called 'negotiators' need to first face these facts squarely, and start from there, and not from 1967 occupation.<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:ThorProvoni@aol.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Joachim Martillo</a> [12/22/2001 4:07:24 AM]<br><br>Zionism is racist because it gives precedence to Jewish ethnic or racial rights over Palestinian human rights.  With the proper substitution one could have made exactly the same point about Nazi ideology.  Nazism is racist because it gives precedence to German ethnic or racial rights over Jewish human rights.<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:tabarani@mailcity.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">A. Mahmoud</a> [12/23/2001 11:06:39 AM]<br><br>In Order to reach reasonable peace agreement we need to keep the following in mind: 1- Asking the Israelis to end the occupation in the same time we ask for ending the Palestinian Uprising -or what's called the violence for 7 days-. 2-Give the right of return to the Palestinians expelled from their land. 3- All religions should have the religious access and right to practice and visit their holy lands. <br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr><tr><td><a href="mailto:imaleh@hotmail.com" class="dead" aria-disabled="true">Iyas Maleh</a> [12/26/2001 11:39:53 PM]<br><br>I am going to diagree with Mr. Ahmed Bouzid here for a second in regard to his admiring the Zionist Occupying Power for their democratic society to quote from the Jewish Defence League website article titled "Jews Deported from Israel": "The great State of Israel. We are all taught that there is a place where Jews can go and be granted sanctuary and instant citizenship, and where we would be embraced into the sodality promised by G-d and our common ancestors. We view Israel as the place where all Jews could go if, G-d forbid, there was another Holocaust; we see it as the nation as where we can be publicly proud of our Judaism, and surrounded by our heritage. I am sorry to report that this land no longer exists, Jews that want to help their people and take a stand against the perversion that spills Jewish blood every day are sent a new message: Stay home. You're not wanted." by Ian Rakow.  From that article we find that some Jews who tried to go to Palestinian Occupied Terretories were descriminated against and sent back to the US for one reason or another.  There are Jews in Prison because they stood up to the Terrorist Government of Ariel Sharon I can provide names from the Amnesty International files... So where is that so called democratic society?  I intentionally did not mention any of their Human Rights abuses against Palestinians (and there are so many of them they would fill volumes) so no one will accuse me of being bias here.  I did not want to mention the Druz who are in prison for their refusal to serve in the Israeli Military.<br><br><br><hr height="1"></td></tr></tbody></table></td></tr>

